Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action gothic period drama, with naturalistic faces, practical candlelight, and oppressive shadow.

FADE IN:

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

A narrow, old-fashioned bedchamber lies in darkness. THE NARRATOR, a lean young man with dark, uncombed hair, hollow cheeks, and a plain black waistcoat, stands at the door.

(SOUND: wind worries the chimney and a clock ticks behind the wall)

NARRATOR V.O.
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

NARRATOR V.O.
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

A frail OLD MAN sleeps beneath a heavy coverlet. His sparse white hair spreads across the pillow; one pale blue eye is shut.

NARRATOR V.O.
I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult.

NARRATOR V.O.
For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye!

The OLD MAN stirs. His closed eyelid trembles.

(SOUND: the clock tick sharpens, then falls away)

NARRATOR V.O.
He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

NARRATOR V.O.
Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so I made up my mind to take the life of the old man.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - MIDNIGHT

The door opens a hair's breadth. THE NARRATOR's black waistcoat and pale face emerge behind it, holding a closed tin lantern.

(SOUND: a latch turns with a tiny metallic click)

NARRATOR V.O.
You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

He eases his head through the opening, inch by inch, never taking his eyes from the sleeping OLD MAN.

(SOUND: floorboards settle under a distant gust)

NARRATOR V.O.
Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

The lantern lid lifts. A single thread of light crosses the bed. The OLD MAN'S eye remains closed.

NARRATOR V.O.
I undid it just so much that a single thin ray fell upon the vulture eye.

NARRATOR V.O.
For seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - MORNING

Gray daylight reaches the bed. THE NARRATOR, lean and neatly buttoned in the same black waistcoat, enters openly with a pleasant smile.

(SOUND: morning sparrows chirp beyond the shuttered window)

The OLD MAN sits up, white-haired and thin beneath his nightshirt. His pale blue eye, filmed and watchful, fixes on THE NARRATOR.

NARRATOR
How have you passed the night?

The OLD MAN gives a small nod. THE NARRATOR pours water at the bedside, steady-handed.

NARRATOR V.O.
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

NARRATOR V.O.
He would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - MIDNIGHT

The shutters are fastened tight against the black night. THE NARRATOR opens the door more slowly than a watch's minute hand.

(SOUND: the lantern hinge gives a dry, reluctant creak)

NARRATOR V.O.
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door.

THE NARRATOR's mouth twitches into a silent chuckle. The OLD MAN jerks upright in bed.

(SOUND: bed ropes strain and the OLD MAN'S breath catches)

OLD MAN
Who's there?

THE NARRATOR freezes behind the door. The lantern closes in his fist.

NARRATOR V.O.
For a whole hour I did not move a muscle.

The OLD MAN remains upright, listening into the dark.

NARRATOR V.O.
He was still sitting up in the bed listening; just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - LATER

THE NARRATOR remains motionless in the doorway. The OLD MAN's white hair and thin shoulders barely rise above the coverlet.

(SOUND: a low, stifled groan comes from the bed)

NARRATOR V.O.
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror.

The OLD MAN grips the blanket to his chin. His open eye searches the black room.

NARRATOR V.O.
It was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe.

(SOUND: a cricket chirps once, then wind hums in the chimney)

NARRATOR V.O.
He had been trying to fancy them causeless, but could not.

NARRATOR V.O.
Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him, and enveloped the victim.

THE NARRATOR cracks the lantern. A dim ray, thin as spider silk, lands squarely on the OLD MAN'S pale blue eye.

(SOUND: the lantern's tin catch trembles against his thumb)

NARRATOR V.O.
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - CONTINUOUS

The ray holds on the filmed blue eye. THE NARRATOR's jaw locks; sweat glistens at his hairline.

(SOUND: a low, dull, quick heartbeat begins beneath the silence)

NARRATOR V.O.
There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

NARRATOR V.O.
I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

THE NARRATOR keeps the lantern motionless. The OLD MAN lies rigid, eye wide, breath fluttering.

(SOUND: the heartbeat grows quicker and louder)

NARRATOR V.O.
The hellish tattoo of the heart increased. It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant.

THE NARRATOR's fingers tighten around the lantern.

NARRATOR V.O.
I thought the heart must burst. A new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour!

He throws open the lantern and leaps into the room.

(SOUND: the OLD MAN gives one piercing shriek)

THE NARRATOR drags the OLD MAN to the floor and heaves the heavy bed over him.

(SOUND: the bed crashes down; the muffled heartbeat continues beneath it)

NARRATOR V.O.
The old man's hour had come!

THE NARRATOR kneels beside the bed, listening. The heartbeat slows, then stops.

(SOUND: the final heartbeat fades into the ticking wall clock)

NARRATOR V.O.
At length it ceased. The old man was dead.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

THE NARRATOR, sleeves rolled above his pale forearms, works by the shuttered window. The OLD MAN'S bed stands empty.

(SOUND: a saw bites softly through wood; a tub receives a measured drip)

NARRATOR V.O.
First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.

Three floor planks lie lifted beside a dark cavity. THE NARRATOR lowers wrapped remains between the scantlings.

NARRATOR V.O.
I then took up three planks from the flooring of the chamber, and deposited all between the scantlings.

He replaces each board, pressing them flush with calm, careful hands.

(SOUND: floorboards settle into place with dull wooden knocks)

NARRATOR V.O.
I replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye could have detected any thing wrong.

A clock bell marks four. THE NARRATOR wipes his hands, buttons his black waistcoat, and smiles.

(SOUND: four deep bell chimes; knocking begins at the street door)

NARRATOR V.O.
When I had made an end of these labors, it was four o'clock—still dark as midnight.

INT. HOUSE ENTRY HALL - PRE-DAWN

THE NARRATOR opens the street door. OFFICER REYNOLDS, broad-shouldered in a dark wool coat, stands with OFFICER HARPER, a narrow man with a trimmed beard, and OFFICER DOYLE, younger and round-faced.

(SOUND: rain taps the front steps and leather boots scrape the threshold)

NARRATOR V.O.
There entered three men, who introduced themselves, with perfect suavity, as officers of the police.

THE NARRATOR welcomes them inside with an open arm. The officers look through the hall, then follow him upstairs.

NARRATOR V.O.
A shriek had been heard by a neighbour during the night; suspicion of foul play had been aroused.

NARRATOR
The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country.

OFFICER REYNOLDS studies THE NARRATOR's face, then gestures for the search to continue.

(SOUND: doors open and close upstairs; drawers slide on their runners)

NARRATOR V.O.
I bade them search—search well.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The room is orderly again. THE NARRATOR leads OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER HARPER, and OFFICER DOYLE inside. The replaced floorboards sit beneath a small circle of chairs.

(SOUND: chair legs scrape the bare wooden floor)

THE NARRATOR displays the OLD MAN'S undisturbed treasures. The officers inspect them, then sit.

NARRATOR V.O.
I showed them his treasures, secure, undisturbed.

THE NARRATOR drags a chair directly over the hidden boards and sits upon it, his black waistcoat immaculate.

NARRATOR V.O.
In the wild audacity of my perfect triumph, I placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse.

The officers relax and converse quietly among themselves. THE NARRATOR answers with a broad, brittle smile.

(SOUND: polite murmurs, a chair creak, and the wall clock ticking)

NARRATOR V.O.
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - DAWN

Weak dawn leaks around the shutters. THE NARRATOR, still lean and black-waistcoated, sits rigidly over the floorboards while the three officers remain in their chairs.

(SOUND: a faint, low, dull, quick beating begins under the floor)

THE NARRATOR's smile drains away. He presses his fingertips to his temple.

NARRATOR V.O.
Ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone.

The officers continue their pleasant conversation, unaware. THE NARRATOR speaks too quickly, gesturing at the treasures.

(SOUND: the beating becomes more distinct beneath the officers' murmurs)

NARRATOR V.O.
I talked more freely to get rid of the feeling; but it continued and gained definiteness.

THE NARRATOR rises and paces with heavy strides. OFFICER DOYLE watches him with mild curiosity.

NARRATOR V.O.
Until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

(SOUND: the heartbeat pounds louder, overtaking the clock)

THE NARRATOR swings his chair and grates it across the boards.

NARRATOR V.O.
I arose and argued about trifles, in a high key and with violent gesticulations; but the noise steadily increased.

The officers exchange small, polite smiles. THE NARRATOR stares at them, breathing through his teeth.

NARRATOR V.O.
Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard! They suspected! They knew!

(SOUND: the heartbeat becomes a relentless, pounding tattoo)

THE NARRATOR lunges toward the floorboards, pointing wildly.

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more!

NARRATOR
I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!

He drops to his knees and claws at the seams between the boards.

(SOUND: nails scrape wood as the heartbeat peaks, then cuts to silence)

NARRATOR
It is the beating of his hideous heart!

FADE OUT.

THE END
